    level: "C1",
    text: "Bar culture in Britain encompasses far more than drinking — it is a social institution woven into the fabric of daily life. The pub (public house) remains the cornerstone: a communal space for after-work pints, Sunday lunches, quiz nights, and community gatherings. 'Your local' — the pub nearest your home — holds a special place in British identity. Ordering at the bar (not table service, traditionally) follows unwritten rules: you wait your turn, the bartender acknowledges you with a nod, and you pay per round. The 'round system' — where each person takes turns buying drinks for the entire group — is a deeply embedded social ritual. Skipping your round is a serious social faux pas. Wine bars emerged in the 1980s as a more sophisticated alternative — catering to a growing interest in wine beyond pub plonk. Cocktail bars followed in the 2000s, bringing craft mixology to British cities. The gastropub — a pub serving restaurant-quality food — blurred the line between drinking and dining. Today's bar landscape includes rooftop bars, hotel bars, craft beer taprooms, natural wine bars, and alcohol-free bars. Licensing laws have evolved: historically, pubs closed at 11pm sharp (the origin of 'last orders'); the 2003 Licensing Act enabled flexible hours, though most still close between midnight and 2am. The rise of 'sober curious' culture means bars now cater to non-drinkers with equal care — quality mocktails, alcohol-free beers, and inclusive menus.",
